<p style=3D"margin: 0;"><span style=3D"font-family: Montserrat, sans-serif;=
">The </span><span style=3D"font-family: Montserrat, sans-serif; font-weigh=
t: bold;">preservation easement</span><span style=3D"font-family: Montserra=
t, sans-serif;"> is one of the only legally enforceable ways to assure a Mo=
dernist house does not get destroyed by future owners. National Register st=
atus? Meaningless. Famous architect? Nope. Local historic designation? At b=
est, a delay in demolition. And as history shows, there are plenty of smili=
ng buyers who say, "I would never tear this down." Then after closing, it's=
 gone. Think of the preservation easement as a homeowner's association for =
one house. The easement requires future owners to maintain the house in cur=
rent structural condition and state of repair, or better, including necessa=
ry work to preserve its integrity.&#xa0;Here's a video featuring Preservati=
on North Carolina and NCModernist, along with </span><span style=3D"font-fa=
mily: Montserrat, sans-serif; font-weight: bold;">Jane Levy</span><span sty=
le=3D"font-family: Montserrat, sans-serif;">, who has an easement on her Gr=
eensboro Modernist home. Here's a sample </span><a href=3D"https://6iwfv6ab=
b.cc.rs6.net/tn.jsp?f=3D001pkiKl0nBbvPXKlWVTZ2xnub-z4RP9P27wKJyo8Rr-MV-wtG2=
G_g7HF6OoOFWbXidhWDi9Sip8968ql5DgsKRu399gLYBVnqgT5T9EQNFq4eE4mkuPIFDdEXooxL=
CH-h3-6zcNSUX2bWV9oVIKevmGEvOyPiH1R8D_7PsloZMdauCFCX0-XTVo-8WrSd0WzCK&c=3Dl=
ilYeW-EfsabOElCupWgx4Ljaae7LWc81TJlJ7bSEVIu4FtpzeGxew=3D=3D&ch=3DzxG-7Rja8g=
EEUeJ5hvCZTQxa3HJLlWW1tYvmesERog1f612Pu_kshA=3D=3D" rel=3D"noopener norefer=
rer" target=3D"_blank" style=3D"font-family: Montserrat, sans-serif; color:=
 #45B3CA; font-weight: bold; text-decoration: none; font-style: normal;">pr=
eservation easement document</a><span style=3D"font-family: Montserrat, san=
s-serif;">. </span></p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0;"><br></p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0;"><span style=3D"font-family: Montserrat, sans-serif;=
">There is another way, the deed restriction. Consider it "preservation eas=
ement lite." When a once-cherished residence falls into the hands of new ow=
ners, there is always the risk that it will be destroyed, despite promises =
to the contrary from those owners.&#xa0;One powerful legal tool families ca=
n use to guard against demolition is a </span><span style=3D"font-family: M=
ontserrat, sans-serif; font-weight: bold;">deed restriction</span><span sty=
le=3D"font-family: Montserrat, sans-serif;">. Knowing that potential buyers=
 can and do lie about destroying houses, a deed restriction is a legally en=
forceable binding promise attached to the property title. Unlike some histo=
ric designations, a deed restriction travels with the property through chan=
ges in ownership. Even if a buyer threatens to demolish or radically alter =
a house, they are limited by the deed. The restriction is usually ten years=
, and it becomes part of the chain of title.&#xa0;Implementing a deed restr=
iction before a sale immediately scares off developers (or developers-in-hi=
ding)</span><span style=3D"font-family: Montserrat, sans-serif; font-weight=
: bold;"> </span><span style=3D"font-family: Montserrat, sans-serif; font-w=
eight: normal;">in an</span><span style=3D"font-family: Montserrat, sans-se=
rif; font-weight: bold;"> </span><span style=3D"font-family: Montserrat, sa=
ns-serif;">owner-value-friendly way to protect a property.&#xa0;</span></p>
